"""Existing indexed capacity-owner expansion; not a new509 guide."""
from refresh_dscr_purchase_methods import refresh,publisher
BODY='''<p class="lead">Before buying an STR, verify that a cleaner can handle the actual peak turnover window—not just an average month or a claimed portfolio size. Accepted crew time, laundry, travel, inspection and backup must fit the same address and opening plan. A low turnover quote does not prove available capacity. For a remote buyer, this is an acquisition-readiness decision: can the proposed operating arrangement serve the property without relying on perfect timing or an unavailable owner?</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the hardest-day capacity worksheet and funded readiness plan to an acquisition call</a>. BNB Accelerator assists within its contracted acquisition scope; this page does not promise cleaning, staffing or ongoing management.</p>
<h2>Verify the buyer's accepted slot before counting crews</h2>
<p>Give the proposed provider the actual address, furnished layout, checklist, intended opening date and permitted turnover window. Ask for accepted dates, named crew or dispatch responsibility, other commitments, report/inspection requirements, laundry method, travel and known unavailable periods. Seller history is useful evidence but does not transfer a service agreement, prepaid balance or place on a route automatically.</p>
<p>Ask how the proposed buyer's jobs are accepted when several properties turn together. A team serving eight homes does not necessarily serve all eight in one afternoon. Two workers traveling together are one crew for this scheduling exercise, not two independently available teams. Establish whether promised staffing is dedicated, shared or subject to later acceptance. Keep uncertainty visible rather than assigning it zero time.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/2895">Airbnb's ground rules for home hosts</a>, reviewed October 8, 2026, require clean listings before check-in and cleaning between stays, including laundry. That platform standard explains why a finished, inspected home matters; it does not certify a cleaner, prescribe a universal turnover duration or promise capacity. This worksheet does not replace professional safety or legal review.</p>
<h2>Original hardest-day acceptance register</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Written evidence</th><th>Buyer decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Available window</td><td>Actual access, departure and promised arrival times, including restrictions</td><td>How much usable time exists for this address?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crew and route</td><td>Accepted staffing, job sequence, other commitments and travel allowance</td><td>Is the same team counted in two places at once?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Work scope</td><td>Property checklist, expected duration, supplies and exclusions</td><td>Does the estimate match the actual furnished layout?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Linen dependency</td><td>Ready inventory, laundry completion, transport and replacement responsibility</td><td>Can clean linens arrive before the final work needs them?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inspection and rework</td><td>Named approver, acceptance evidence and correction path</td><td>Who decides the home is ready rather than merely cleaned?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fallback and price</td><td>Separately accepted backup, activation terms, charges and unavailable periods</td><td>Does backup share the same staffing, laundry or road bottleneck?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Record dates, source documents and unanswered questions. Ask for references through authorized channels and an agreed trial where access permits. Protect occupied stays, seller consent and guest privacy. A successful trial documents one event; it does not guarantee a future holiday. The <a href="/blog/backup-cleaner-str/">backup acceptance guide</a> covers replacement-provider terms and incremental costs; this page isolates simultaneous route and material capacity.</p>
<h2>Worked route: spare crew minutes do not fix late linens</h2>
<p>All durations below are invented, not market benchmarks, vendor promises or recommended service times. Assume a 240-minute window after authorized access. One accepted crew has two sequential jobs, each requiring 85 crew-minutes of onsite work, 15 minutes of inspection and 20 minutes of travel between homes. Work plus inspections plus travel is 170 + 30 + 20 = 220 minutes, leaving only 20 unassigned minutes. Worker labor-hours are not the same as elapsed crew time.</p>
<p>One simplified route starts job one at minute 0, finishes its work at 85 and inspection at 100, travels until 120, finishes job two at 205 and inspection at 220. Stipulate ready supplies and first-job linens, no breaks or extra work, and an independently available inspector at the specified times. The sequence is mathematically feasible under those limited assumptions, not evidence that it will be performed or that either home is safe to open.</p>
<p>Now separate job two's final ten minutes of linen placement from its first 75 minutes of other work. Those ten minutes are already inside the 85-minute job estimate, not additional labor. The crew reaches the linen dependency at minute 195. If clean linens are available then, placement ends at 205 and inspection at 220. If linens arrive only at 235, placement ends at 245 and inspection at 260—20 minutes beyond the 240-minute window. The original 20-minute margin did not absorb the 40-minute wait.</p>
<p>More cleaners do not automatically solve that material delay. Test genuinely available ready inventory, a different accepted laundry path or a valid different schedule, with actual costs and terms. Do not assume a guest will accept a changed arrival time or manufacture a refund estimate to make the plan work. In the same simplified two-job-per-crew pattern, two independent crews could cover four jobs, not eight; each additional route still needs its own accepted resources and inspection. Portfolio counts are not simultaneous capacity.</p>
<h2>Bridge only distinct readiness changes into purchase cash</h2>
<p>Separate invented cash example, not a continuation of the route's workload or a provider quote: $320,000 is genuinely available. Purchase, closing, setup and a protected $40,000 reserve already allocate $301,000, leaving $19,000 unassigned. Assume three distinct readiness costs excluded from that total: a $900 authorized trial, $1,800 additional ready-linen stock and a $600 accepted capacity reservation payment that credits no jobs. They total $3,300; allocation becomes $304,300 and margin $15,700.</p>
<p>The original allocation also includes two $220 ordinary turns. If two accepted $320 replacement turns take their place, add two times the $100 difference, or $200—not another $640 on top of the original allowance. Allocation becomes $304,500 with $15,500 margin. A separate $1,800 opening-delay obligation, if actually payable and not included elsewhere, increases allocation to $306,300 and leaves $13,700. No lost revenue, platform remedy or reimbursement is assumed.</p>
<p>Retained reserve is allocated once and is not automatically available for these additions. Earmarked costs are not necessarily paid on day one; reconcile invoices, due dates and cleared funds using the <a href="/tools/first-str-purchase-budget/">purchase-budget worksheet</a>. Do not add linens already included in setup again or confuse a reservation payment with completed work. These partial numbers establish neither adequate reserves nor investment profit. Expected bookings and tax refunds are not cleared purchase cash.</p>
<h2>Choose the purchase path with readiness still visible</h2>
<p>Proceed when the address-specific route, materials, inspection and fallback have accepted owners and the downside is independently funded. Reprice a viable acquisition when verified readiness costs change. Delay opening or seek valid transaction protection while decisive acceptance remains missing; counsel determines contractual rights. Reject a rental-ready thesis that depends on overlapping crews, unavailable linens or an owner who cannot cover the remaining duty.</p>
<p>Keep cleaning separate from <a href="/blog/str-maintenance-response-sla/">physical repair response</a> and <a href="/blog/after-hours-guest-support-cost/">guest messaging</a>. A cleaner's accepted work does not assign those tasks. Follow the <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income buyer roadmap</a> and <a href="/apply/">compare candidates using accepted peak-day capacity and dated cash</a>.</p>
<p class="small">BNB Accelerator is the interested acquisition-service publisher. Document-based education, not firsthand vendor testing, a rating or professional legal, safety, tax or investment advice. All numerical assumptions are hypothetical. Keep guest, seller, vendor and access records in authorized secure channels. No capacity, response time, opening date, tax benefit or investment result is guaranteed.</p>'''
FAQ=[('Does a cleaner’s portfolio size prove peak-day capacity?','No. Check accepted crews, overlapping commitments, route time, work scope, materials, inspection and the actual turnover window for this address.'),('Can extra crew time solve unavailable linens?','Not automatically. Map the material dependency and remaining work. A feasible labor total can still miss readiness when clean linens arrive too late.'),('Does a successful trial guarantee future holiday availability?','No. It documents that agreed event, not future capacity. Obtain accepted dates, activation terms and a fallback that addresses shared dependencies.'),('Should replacement cleaning be added in full to an existing allowance?','Not when it replaces the same already-budgeted work. Reconcile the incremental difference and add distinct trial, linen or capacity costs once, with actual payment dates.')]
